 sjw@liberty.demon.co.uk  (Stephen J. Whitrow) writes in X-Message-Number: #94-6-22:
>I'd like to add a couple of points from a TV programme I saw a while back,
>on the importance of bipedalism to brain evolution.  Since bipedalism left
>the hands free to develop tools etc, this fed back to the brain.  Those who
>engaged in tasks which developed dexterity were exercising their brains
>more compared to the quadrupeds who were unable to, and this promoted an
>increase in brain size. 

 That was widely believed at one time. C. Owen Lovejoy, in "Evolution of
Human Walking", Scientific American, November 1988, provides strong
evidence that human ancestors were fully bipedal 3 million years ago,
0.5 million years before the increase in brain size and tool use started.
Lovejoy suggests that the advantage of bipedalism was that it freed the
hands to carry food over long distances. Bipedalism may have been necessary
for increased brain size to be useful, but the fact that bipedalism evolved
in time to be used for that purpose is an accident.

>But bigger brains consume more power, requiring a more efficient cooling
>system (this evolution occurred in the East Rift valley of Africa).  An

 Don't assume that evolution occurred where the most evidence is found.
Bones are preserved much better in some areas than in others (glaciers
are very effective at wiping out evidence). Calvin likens the focus on
Africa to the who looks for his lost keys where the light is best rather
than where he might have lost them.
